From 60caa7fc6852568f8f25f285c57c893fde244118 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jurilw <washington.juri@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 13:41:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] fix(persistence): import V1 threads created after the V2
 snapshot

statev2.sqlite is seeded from state.sqlite only when it does not exist.
After V2 -> V1 (0.0.x) -> V2, every thread made in the V1 build lives only
in state.sqlite, and V2 never reads that file again: the threads vanish
from the sidebar although nothing is lost. (Seen on a real install: 0.1.0
created statev2 on 10-20, 0.0.45 builds then added 4 threads and 6
projects, 0.1.1 showed none of them.)

On startup, when both files exist, copy into statev2's legacy projection
tables the V1 projects, threads, messages and pull-request links whose ids
V2 has never seen; LegacyV1ThreadImporter then imports them exactly like
snapshot threads (shells at startup, transcripts lazily).

- Never modifies state.sqlite (opened read-only, like the snapshot).
- Skips V1-deleted threads and anything V2 already has; edits to threads
  V2 already has are not merged, V2 stays authoritative.
- Records copied ids in t3code_legacy_v1_resync, so a thread deleted in V2
  is not resurrected on the next start.
- A V1 project whose workspace root (Windows paths case- and
  slash-insensitive) matches a live V2 project is not duplicated; its
  threads move to the V2 project.
- Copies only columns both schemas have; one transaction; failures are
  logged and never block startup.

Tests: legacyV1Resync.test.ts (V1 -> V2 -> V1 edits -> V2, through the
real migrations and importer). Also checked by hand against a copy of the
affected install: all 4 threads and their 125 user/assistant messages
imported, 14_Orchestrator merged into the existing V2 project.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
